 {"q": "Why did the Athenians choose most officials by lot rather than by election?", "options": ["They could not count votes accurately", "They believed the gods chose through the lot", "Elections favour the rich, famous and persuasive; the lot gives every citizen an equal chance", "Officials had no real power"], "answer": 2, "why": "Aristotle says the lot is thought democratic and election oligarchic; equality of chance was the point."},
 {"q": "The generals were elected, not chosen by lot. What does this show?", "options": ["That Athens was secretly an aristocracy", "That the Athenians knew some jobs needed skill and experience too much to leave to chance", "That generals were always rich", "That the lot had been abolished"], "answer": 1, "why": "Where expertise mattered most, war and large sums of money, the Athenians used election."},
 {"q": "Ostracism was designed against which danger?", "options": ["Foreign spies", "Corrupt judges", "A popular leader becoming a tyrant, as Peisistratus had", "Rich men refusing to pay taxes"], "answer": 2, "why": "The law was passed 'as a precaution against men in high office, because Pisistratus took advantage of his position as a popular leader... to make himself tyrant'."},
